One of the main arguments made by these experts is that South Korea had been secretly planning to invade North Korea for months before the war began. They point to the fact that South Korea had been building up its military capabilities and had been conducting military exercises near the border with North Korea in the months leading up to the war. They also argue that South Korea had been receiving military aid and advice from the United States, which had a strong interest in preventing the spread of communism in East Asia.

